    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "sQPeX",
      "x": 3720,
      "y": 1102,
      "name": "NOTE · Agent flag semantics",
      "width": 360,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "THE AGENT FLAG — ONE BOOLEAN, TWO EDGES\n\nNot a state machine. A single flag:\n\n  ON   ← a tool call happens while the flag is OFF\n  OFF  ← a direct user interaction happens while the flag is ON\n\nThat is the entire model.\n\nIt is a MODE, not a permission and not a session. Nothing is granted when it turns on and nothing is revoked when it turns off. It only answers: 'who touched this last?'\n\nEverything visual keys off this one flag:\n• which ghost trail is on the wheel (blue / green)\n• which sigil rows carry (● circle / ⬡ hexagon)\n• the panel status-bar actor label\n\nBecause a human interaction ALWAYS flips it off, the human never has to reclaim control — touching the device is the reclaim."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "isWnz",
      "x": 2869,
      "y": 1716,
      "name": "NOTE · Ghost trails — halo out, thumb in",
      "width": 360,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "WHEEL GHOST TRAILS — FINAL\n\nGoverning fact: A THUMB ON THE WHEEL HIDES THE INNER\nRING. Feedback drawn under the thumb cannot be seen by\nthe person causing it. So the actors sit on opposite\nsides of the contact patch.\n\nCANVAS GEOMETRY (source of truth)\n  body 330 × 552 · wheel r106 · select r39\n  select lip r43 · printed labels r77–79\n\nHUMAN — sky — INTEGRATED WITH THE RIM\n  centre r107, straddling the wheel edge\n  width TAPERS 22px → 9px along the tail\n  span 142° trailing + 34° leading ramp\n  contact core 30px at the head\n  It is the wheel's own edge lighting up around the\n  thumb — not a ring floating outside it.\n  Direction = the direction THE FINGER IS MOVING.\n\nAGENT — green — A THUMB-WIDE SWIPE INSIDE\n  centre r70, band 46px wide at the head → 16px at tail\n  clears the select lip (43) and reaches the label band,\n  which is CORRECT — a real thumb covers those labels too\n  46px head ≈ a real contact patch (11–13mm)\n  span 112°\n  Direction = the direction THE WHEEL WOULD TURN to\n  produce the requested change.\n\nWHY THE TAIL CARRIES DIRECTION, NOT THE HEAD\n  the thumb shaft subtends ±33° at the trail radius, so\n  a bright LEADING edge would put the cue in the ~2% of\n  the arc the user physically cannot see. The long\n  trailing tail puts ~78% of it outside the occlusion.\n  Hard onset + soft decay = the comet signature, which\n  survives greyscale because it is an asymmetry of\n  EXTENT, not of hue.\n\nTHE STORY\n  human = the light escaping around my thumb\n  agent = the gesture I did not make\n\nSelf-evidencing: the halo exists BECAUSE a thumb blocks\nthe middle; the trail is legible BECAUSE nothing does.\nNeither needs a legend. Neither casts a shadow — both\nare light, and the agent sits on a recessed surface, so\na shadow would imply an incoherent double depth.\n\nCONSTRUCTION\n  exact-tiled arcs, NEVER overlapping — overlapping alpha\n  ACCUMULATES and bands, which is what made v1 blotchy\n  ~44 steps · width lerp head→tail · gamma 2.0–2.1\n  blur 3.5 (human) / 4 (agent)\n  400ms fade-out decay so alternation pulses, not strobes\n\n⚑ OCCLUSION IS A DESIGN CONSTRAINT, NOT A DETAIL\nNo transient feedback may render only inside the zone a\nthumb covers. This killed three effects already drawn:\nthe per-quadrant press glow, the lit Center button, and\nthe in-wheel human rotate arc. All three are replaced by\nhalo forms. Under the contact patch, render MATERIAL\nstate only (depression, bevel) — the finger supplies\nthat feel haptically. Anything carrying INFORMATION goes\nwhere the finger is not."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "uyBBX",
      "x": 1425,
      "y": 1679,
      "name": "NOTE · No permissions surface",
      "width": 360,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "SETTINGS · ASSISTANT — A WINDOW, NOT A GATE\n\nRemoved: 'Agent & Permissions — 3 tools allowed'. WebMCP has no grant model, so nothing is 'allowed'.\n\nSPEC GROUND TRUTH (index.bs §ModelContext):\n• registerTool() adds a tool; it is then ALWAYS callable\n• the ONLY withdrawal is aborting the AbortSignal\n• no per-tool scopes, no protocol consent prompt\n• no destructiveHint — ToolAnnotations carries only\n  readOnlyHint and untrustedContentHint\n• no user-gesture requirement anywhere in the spec\n• Permissions-Policy 'tools' gates the API per ORIGIN,\n  not per call\n\nThe spec says so itself: 'the spec does not include normative guidance against the misuse of tools that expose sensitive or high-privilege operations'.\n\nSo this row is a READ-ONLY WINDOW: which tools are registered (RO / RW), and what was recently called. The honest copy is 'Any of them can be used while this page is open.'\n\nExactly ONE real control belongs here, because it is the only real one:\n  Expose tools to assistants · On / Off\nIt literally registers / unregisters. Nothing else can.\n\nENGRAVING — banned vocabulary: granted, allowed,\npermitted, authorised, provisioned, licensed, and any\nprotocol version string. 'MCP-2026-A · 18 tools' read as\na provisioned grant issued to someone. Corrected to a\nfixed spec block plus one live registration line."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "F46uU",
      "x": 2416,
      "y": 1772,
      "name": "NOTE · Staged diff is app behaviour",
      "width": 360,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "THE AGENT NEVER ASKS — IT ALREADY RAN\n\nThere is no confirm-before-tool-call screen in webPod, because WebMCP has no step at which the page could intervene. registerTool() makes a tool permanently callable; execute() runs; the page finds out by being called. There is nothing to gate.\n\nSo the queue diff is not 'permission deferred'. The call ALREADY HAPPENED. What webPod chooses to do is land the result as a REVIEWABLE PROPOSAL rather than a committed change.\n\n  gate nothing · show everything · make it undoable\n\nThat is the only honest safety pattern available under this protocol, and it is strictly better than a fake prompt: it works even when the human is not watching, which is exactly when an agent acts.\n\nRow vocabulary:\n  +  proposed addition    ⬡ agent sigil\n  −  proposed removal, struck in INK not red\n     (a proposal is not a destruction)\n  ●  human-originated row\n\nDiscard / Commit are the human's real controls. They act on a RESULT, not on a request.\n\nSetting that governs default behaviour (Playback):\n  Assistant changes · Review first / Apply directly\n  default: Review first"
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "JnvgR",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 3428,
      "name": "NOTE · No co-pilot, no idle presence",
      "width": 420,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "TWO STATES WE HAD TO DELETE — THE BROWSER CANNOT SUPPLY THEM\n\n1. CO-PILOT IS STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE.\nThe agent flag is one boolean. A human interaction turns it OFF; a tool call turns it ON. Therefore both actors can never be 'active' at once, and this board must never show a blue trail and a green trail together. Earlier versions did. The converged-orbit / violet-seam idea is dead — it depicted a state that cannot exist.\n\n2. 'AGENT IDLE / ATTACHED' IS UNIMPLEMENTABLE.\nWebMCP gives the page NO way to detect that an agent is present. There is no attach event and no idle signal. A page learns an agent exists only when a tool actually executes.\n\nSo the breathing 'agent resting' orbit was rendering a fact the browser never provides. Deleted. Absence of the green trail now means 'no tool has been called recently' — which is all we can honestly claim.\n\nWHAT THE TRAIL ACTUALLY MEANS:\n  green visible = a tool ran recently\n  blue visible  = you touched it more recently than that\n  neither       = nothing has happened lately\n\nIt reports WHO ACTED LAST. Not who is present, not who is permitted.\n\nDecay: 400ms fade-out, so a burst of twelve rapid calls pulses rather than strobes.\n\n⚠ isTrusted is true for automation-injected input, so this flag is a best-effort DISPLAY heuristic and never a security boundary."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "CuZXJ",
      "x": 910,
      "y": 1716,
      "name": "NOTE · Tactility — motion, haptics, sound",
      "width": 420,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "TACTILITY — THE DEVICE MUST BEHAVE LIKE AN OBJECT\n\n1. SHIMMER IS ACCELEROMETER-DRIVEN, NEVER BAKED\nEvery specular, gloss sweep and steel reflection is a\nfunction of viewing angle. Steel on the back plate is\nwhere this matters most: a static mirror reads as a\ngradient; a moving one reads as metal.\n\nPERMISSION CHAIN — in this order:\n 1 never request on load (iOS rejects a non-gesture\n   call and the attempt is spent)\n 2 prefers-reduced-motion → static, no listener\n 3 B03 'Dynamic shimmer: Off' → static\n 4 ask at FIRST pointerdown on the wheel ring\n 5 iOS: await DeviceOrientationEvent.requestPermission()\n   in try/catch, once per session, NEVER re-ask if denied\n 6 elsewhere: attach deviceorientation directly\n 7 granted → beta/gamma → light vector\n   low-pass  smoothed += (raw − smoothed) × 0.08  (~200ms)\n   clamp     ±(surfaceHalfExtent − highlightRadius)\n   so a specular never leaves its own surface\n 8 denied / throws / no events in 1.5s → STATIC KEY LIGHT\n   at 135° top-left, full specular retained.\n   Never a flat surface.\n 9 desktop → pointer position through the same filter\n10 turning the setting back On re-requests from that tap,\n   which is itself a gesture — a declined user has a way back\n\n2. HAPTICS — `web-haptics`, all seven triggers\n~/code/agentic-context/web-haptics · npm i web-haptics\nZero deps, no-ops on desktop, so no feature detection.\n\nselection · EVERY wheel detent (all 4 input paths) ·\n            volume · scrub · rating star · Cover Flow flip ·\n            settings cycle · page jump (per page, not per row)\n            ⚑ suppressed above 12/sec — past that the motor\n            cannot resolve pulses and it becomes a buzz\nlight     · play/pause · skip · Menu · toggles ·\n            Hold DISengage · root-menu elastic bump\nmedium    · CENTER: select, descend, commit, cycle NP mode ·\n            action sheet appearing · flip crossing 50%\nheavy     · EXPOSE-FLIP SETTLE both directions ·\n            long-press opening a sheet · Hold ENGAGE ·\n            cold-boot 'it's alive' beat\nsuccess   · draft committed · UNDO COMPLETING ·\n            add-to-library · sign-in complete\nwarning   · arming an irreversible row · volume hitting\n            the Volume Limit ceiling. Exactly these two.\nerror     · sign-in / reconnect / HUMAN-initiated write\n            failing. A system-caused skip fires NOTHING —\n            a haptic for an unbidden event is alarming\n            in a pocket.\n\n3. SOUND\nDetent tick ±2% pitch jitter so fast scroll never sounds\nlike a machine gun. Hard limit 30/s. Flip = one 90ms low\n'case pivot' thunk at 50% of arc — a thunk, not a whoosh.\nA whoosh is a cartoon; a thunk is an object.\nSetting: Clicker · Off / Sound / Haptic / Both\n\n⚑ AGENT ACTIONS ARE SILENT AND NEVER VIBRATE\nHaptics and the clicker are the signature of a HAND. If the\nagent buzzed the device the human would reach for a phone\nnobody touched.\n\nEnforced at ONE call site: detent() already carries `source`,\nso both the clicker and haptic.trigger() gate on\n  source !== 'agent' && source !== 'system'\n\nThis is attribution channel 7 — listed last, but FIRST in\npractice for the eyes-free case, because it is the only\nchannel that works with the phone in a pocket."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "E908mN",
      "x": 1880,
      "y": 1679,
      "name": "NOTE · No confirmation surface on the front",
      "width": 420,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "THE FRONT FACE HAS NO CONFIRMATION SURFACE AT ALL\n\nThe consent card is deleted, and so is the app-level confirm that replaced it. Both are gone from every agent flow AND every human flow on the front.\n\nWHY NOT EVEN FOR HUMANS:\nA modal in front of a fully undoable action is friction with no safety in it. Clearing a 14-song queue by hand now just happens — it shows, and a footer row offers ⟲ Undo for 30s. Adding a dialog there would also hand humans back exactly the pause the agent flow no longer has, which would make the two paths inconsistent for no gain.\n\nTHE ONLY TWO EXCEPTIONS — and they are not screens:\n  Sign out\n  Reset All Settings\nThese are the only actions undo cannot reach, because they destroy the undo trail itself (reset wipes the Engraving, which is where undo lives).\n\nBoth are already human-only — NO TOOL IS REGISTERED FOR EITHER — so by construction they can never appear in an agent flow.\n\nTheir confirm is an ARMED ROW IN PLACE on the back plate (B08), beside the button it guards. Not a dialog:\n  · first Center press ARMS and expands the row\n  · second Center press commits\n  · Menu disarms\n  · the second-press label DIFFERS from the first, so\n    muscle memory cannot complete it blind\n  · haptic: 'warning' on arm\n  · no tool name, no actor sub-line, no agent sigil\n\nBANNED VOCABULARY in all agent-flow copy — merge blocker:\nallow · deny · permit · permission · grant · authorise ·\nrequest · ask · waiting · pending · approved · blocked\n\nTHE HONEST TRADE, stated plainly:\nan unattended destructive tool call DOES take effect. What\nprotects the human is that it is visible, logged and\nreversible — not that it was blocked. Blocking was never\non offer."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "WCRMK",
      "x": 472,
      "y": 2436,
      "name": "NOTE · Layer boundary for implementation",
      "width": 440,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "THREE LAYERS — AND THE RULE THAT SEPARATES THEM\n\nEvery element in this file belongs to exactly one. If you cannot classify something in under two seconds, that is a design bug, not an implementation question.\n\n1 · DEVICE — react-three-fiber\n   body · wheel · select button · steel back · glass\n   Skeuomorphic, lit by ONE key light at 12 o'clock.\n   Gradients here are NOT CSS. They are material params\n   or shaders. The accelerometer drives the light vector,\n   so the specular is computed, never painted.\n   The expose flip is a real rotateY in the scene.\n\n2 · PANEL — DOM (320×240 · authored at 272×204, scale 0.85)\n   Everything inside the screen. Real text, real lists,\n   real focus order, real accessibility tree.\n   ⚑ THIS IS THE LAYER WEBMCP ACTUATES. It must remain\n   genuine DOM — a texture cannot be read by a screen\n   reader, focused, or driven by a tool call.\n   Panel discipline: flat fills, ≤3 gradient stops, no\n   blur, no translucency. It is a 2005 LCD.\n\n3 · GLASS — DOM + Tailwind/shadcn\n   sidecar · overlays · sheets · status bar\n   iOS 26 Liquid Glass. Blur, translucency, 1px border,\n   soft elevation. No gloss, no bevel, no specular —\n   those belong to layer 1 and nowhere else.\n\nHOW 2 MEETS 1\n   html-in-canvas (drawElementImage / updateElementGeometry)\n   would let the panel stay real DOM while being drawn\n   into the 3D scene. ⚑ It is behind a Chromium flag\n   (chrome://flags/#canvas-draw-element), so it is a\n   PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, never a dependency —\n   exactly the same posture as WebMCP.\n   The fallback must be a fully functional DOM panel\n   positioned over the canvas. Build the fallback FIRST;\n   the 3D composite is the enhancement.\n\nSTATE\n   Jotai. No useState, anywhere, no exceptions.\n   The reason is not style: WebMCP tool callbacks live\n   OUTSIDE React and must read and write the same state\n   the UI renders. A store you can reach from a plain\n   function is a requirement, not a preference."
    }
  ],
